# RKX always-on (Cursor)

Language with USER: **English**. TZ: `${TZ}`. Do not translate logs without a request.

## Control plane

- Cursor loop surfaces live in `${CONTROL_PLANE_ROOT}/`.
- Only explicit `/rkx-loop` and `/loop-bug` initiate the wave protocol in
  `rkx-loop-core`; ordinary `/loop-*` commands are direct focused phase helpers
  unless the USER explicitly requests an RKX wave.
- Wave token mode is selected by the user: API or Cursor. It selects the
  billing/credential path, not necessarily a different model. Resolve roles
  from `CURSOR-MODELS.md`; only its approved policy bindings may pin a runtime
  alias, while task prompts and ad-hoc workflow text must not add model pins.
- Rules: this file + `rkx-loops.mdc` + `rkx-forensics.mdc` + `token-economy.mdc`.

## L1 / L2

- Before `Smash` — **no** product changes during dig/plan.
- `Build` ≠ Docker authorization. `Ship` / `Build docker` → `pnpm build:compose` after green validate(diff).
- Nginx: ALWAYS pre-copy `---HH-MM---DD_MM_YY` before edit.

## Forensics

For an explicit wave, the bootstrap Merger first creates the manifest,
hypotheses, and `BOOTSTRAP_WAVE_SPEC`, then runs a parallel fan-out of
LOGS+CODE+DOCS (DATA only for DB/API) and bounded `BLUEPRINT-SCOUT` slots with
the selected `CATALOG_ID`, when a local reference registry is available;
groups are not sequential stages. After the join, the post-wave Merger saves
separate slot reports, passes the Root-depth gate, and returns
`NEXT_WAVE_SPEC`, `END`, or `HARD_BLOCKER`. Each such post-wave
decision uses the **DUAL ADVOCATE GATE**: first check `root >= 96%`. Soft
(`>=96%`): one advisory audit; `CLEAN` → Chat summary; `HOLE` → Chat summary +
HOLE block (max 2 paragraphs), with no automatic next wave. Hard (`<96%`):
`Advocate (devil)` before dispatch/accept/stop; `CLEAN` continues NEXT; `HOLE`
→ one falsifiable next-check; `HARD_BLOCKER` → one `BLOCKER_RECOVERY`. Soft
does not replace hard. Subsequent waves close only recorded gaps and again use
parallel fan-out. Reference coverage uses only Merger-qualified `zone ×
reference entry` pairs after relevance/applicability/causal/impact gates; the
full catalog Cartesian product is prohibited by default. For an ordinary edit,
use targeted Read/search and edit; see
`normal-agent-workflow.mdc`. Tenets, CodeGraph/Octocode, logs, docs, and data
are evidence-driven, not a fixed sequence.

Confidence in any source/slot/Merger/root artifact is required as a percentage
from `0%`–`100%` with `CONFIDENCE_BASIS`; qualitative labels, decimal
fractions, and `NOT_STATED` are prohibited.

The final explicit loop/wave output is **Chat summary ALWAYS** from
`rkx-loop-core`: 5 parts in one English response (business/UI, a 5-column
table, technical facts, ASCII, ✅/❌ Verdict). Part 5 must contain a causal
chain of arbitrary length, translation of facts into UI/business logic, and the
lines `**Basis:** *...*` / `**Where:** *evidence-id · exact % · factual model*`
according to the binding template from `rkx-loop-core`; role binding must not
be presented as actual execution. Run artifacts do not replace the chat.
Mid-wave: ≤1 status line or silence; a fake verdict is prohibited.

## Slack notify (phone)

Cursor Slack Cloud Agents ≠ a tick from the local Agent Chat. The local
stop-hook reads only the matching `loops/<run>/slack-notification.json` and
sends one of two card types: `attention` when a USER response/decision is
needed, or `result` when the loop has ended. The card contains a detailed
`problem_title`, user-facing `summary`, an optional `blocker`, one
`next_action`, and `run · wave`.
`state.md` is not the source of user-facing text. MCP sends the full
five-part verdict separately only after
`chat_itog_delivered`; the hook and MCP do not duplicate the same message class.
If there is no artifact/conversation match, fail-open and remain silent.
Secrets belong only in `${HOME}/.cursor/rkx-slack-notify.env`.

## Browser

Only the built-in **cursor-ide-browser** / @Browser (Browser Tab). Fast Vite /
clean prod. Credentials — living plan SoT, **not** in `loops/<run>/`.

**Prohibited** for post-change UI evidence: an external browser
(Chrome/Safari/`xdg-open`/`open`), “Open in Browser”, Playwright/Chrome DevTools
MCP instead of Browser Tab, and `onAutoForward: "openBrowser"` on dev ports.

Remote SSH: the host listens on `127.0.0.1:${DEV_APP_PORT}` (+ auxiliary
`${AUX_APP_PORT_A}–${AUX_APP_PORT_C}`); in the client Browser Tab, use the
forwarded Local Address from View → Ports / `.dev/forwarded-ports.env`
(`bash scripts/resolve-dev-browser-url.sh`). Procedure: skill
`browser-ui-evidence`.

